{
    "attributes": {
        "action": "Scrap the planned increase in Income Tax on rental property income",
        "additional_details": "Labour promised in its 2024 manifesto not to increase Income Tax rates. Yet the Labour Government has now introduced separate, higher rates on rental property income, reaching 24%, 44% and 49% from April 2027. This increases the tax burden on landlords who already face rising costs. In our view, it also raises questions about manifesto accountability and fairness. Higher taxation could discourage private landlords, reduce rental housing supply and put further pressure on tenants. We want equal tax treatment for all income.",
        "background": "Scrap the planned increase in Income Tax on rental property income. Before the Election Labour promised not to raise Income Tax rates yet the Government has now introduced higher rates for landlords. We think this undermines the spirit of its manifesto commitment & unfairly penalises property owners",
